 \begin{abstract}
-We study rational-circle systems arising from the four pairs of opposite entries of a $3\times3$ magic square of distinct positive integer squares. For a primitive central root $y=p^kq^\ell$, local valuations restrict the four frequency vectors to a two-parameter normal form. We give exact certificates excluding every such rational-circle configuration with $k+\ell\le7$, and hence obtain the necessary condition $k+\ell\ge8$. The certificates include all signed assignments, exceptional fibers, and projective parameter values. For a three-parameter configuration, retaining the rational normalization of a unit-circle direction imposes two simultaneous square conditions. Three elementary elliptic descents exclude forty of its forty-eight signed systems. The remaining existence problem is equivalent to the existence of a rational point outside eight specified points on an explicit genus-five curve; rational reconstruction formulas prove the converse. Integer monomial maps transfer an exclusion of this configuration to three other configurations. The genus-five point list is not proved complete. The results concern specified subfamilies of the rational-circle relaxation and do not decide the unrestricted magic-square problem.
+We study rational-circle systems arising from the four pairs of opposite entries of a $3\times3$ magic square of distinct positive integer squares. For a primitive central root $y=p^kq^\ell$, local valuations restrict the four frequency vectors to a two-parameter normal form. We give exact certificates excluding every such rational-circle configuration with $k+\ell\le7$, and hence obtain the necessary condition $k+\ell\ge8$. The certificates include all signed assignments, exceptional fibers, and projective parameter values. For a three-parameter configuration, retaining the rational normalization of a unit-circle direction imposes two simultaneous square conditions. Sixteen of its forty-eight signed systems are immediately degenerate, and three elementary elliptic descents exclude another twenty-four. The remaining existence problem is equivalent to the existence of a rational point outside eight specified points on an explicit genus-five curve; rational reconstruction formulas prove the converse. Integer monomial maps transfer an exclusion of this configuration to three other configurations. The genus-five point list is not proved complete. The results concern specified subfamilies of the rational-circle relaxation and do not decide the unrestricted magic-square problem.
 \end{abstract}
