 \subsection{Relation to prior work and scope}
 The local valuation formula and the fact that at least three opposite-pair differences are units at each center prime appear in Sections~3--4 of the public \emph{Magic Squares of Squares} research archive~\cite{MSSArchive}. We cite that argument and include a proof to specify exactly where primitivity enters. A separate note in the archive claims a uniform two-prime exclusion for actual integer squares~\cite{MSSTwoBlock}. That claim is not an assumption of this paper. Our finite exclusion theorem has the larger domain $\T(\Q)^2$ with specified frequencies, whereas the original arithmetic problem imposes additional Gaussian-prime conditions.
